Session 3, 27.10.2026

Today we focused on analog signals, inputs, and serial communication. 
We explored Pulse-Width Modulation (PWM) via analogWrite(), utilizing the duty cycle (the proportion of on-time in each cycle) 
to control things like LED brightness smoothly.

I also learned how to read sensor data:

Digital & Analog Inputs: We tested digitalRead() using a tilt switch and used INPUT_PULLUP to prevent floating pins.

Analog-to-Digital Conversion: Using analogRead() on analog pins (A0–A5), 
an internal Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) translates an analog voltage (0–5V) 
into digital values ranging from 0 to 1023.

Components: We wired a potentiometer (acting as a voltage divider/adjustable resistor) and a photoresistor (LDR), 
whose resistance changes depending on light intensity.

Serial Monitor: We used Serial.begin(9600) and Serial.print() to read inputs and debug values directly in the Serial Monitor.

For setting up serial communication and reading inputs/fading LEDs, we worked with code like this:

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); 
  delay(1000); 
  Serial.println("Type something:");
}

void loop() {
  if (Serial.available()) { 
    String str = Serial.readString();
    str.trim();
  }
}

And for fading an LED using a for loop:

for (int i = 0; i <= 255; i += 5) {
  analogWrite(3, i);
  delay(30);
}

